WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.380
- [Lecturer] So I'm Marzyeh Ghassemi

00:00:01.380 --> 00:00:03.810
and I'm a professor at MIT.

00:00:03.810 --> 00:00:04.860
I'm also an affiliate

00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:07.860
of the Vector Institute
and University of Toronto.

00:00:07.860 --> 00:00:09.840
And today I'm going to talk to you about

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:12.090
The Pulse of Ethical
Machine Learning in Health.

00:00:12.090 --> 00:00:13.380
And I should warn you,

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:16.920
I had a talk that is a tight hour.

00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:19.560
I had it all, I've given it 50 times

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:21.010
and this is a brand new talk.

00:00:21.848 --> 00:00:24.870
So you are either blessed or
cursed with being the first

00:00:24.870 --> 00:00:29.310
people to hear this
particular set of slides.

00:00:29.310 --> 00:00:34.020
So my group at MIT is the
Healthy Machine Learning Lab

00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:37.680
and we try to create actionable
insights in human health.

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:40.200
And here's a case study
of what you might do

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:42.390
if you're working in
machine learning and health.

00:00:42.390 --> 00:00:44.790
So let's say that you
have a patient who comes

00:00:44.790 --> 00:00:47.280
into the hospital, Sumana's
having trouble breathing,

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:50.220
she goes to the emergency
room, they do a chest x-ray,

00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:52.920
but the doctor can't look at
the chest x-ray for two hours

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:55.290
because there are lots of
people in the emergency room.

00:00:55.290 --> 00:00:58.410
But if she's healthy, she would
really like to just go home.

00:00:58.410 --> 00:01:01.440
Can we build a model to triage her?

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:04.530
Well, if we wanted to focus on this,

00:01:04.530 --> 00:01:07.410
there is a pipeline that we
work with in machine learning

00:01:07.410 --> 00:01:10.500
generally where we select
a problem, identify data,

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:14.340
collect an outcome, label,
develop an algorithm,

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:15.390
and then finally look

00:01:15.390 --> 00:01:18.120
at post appointment
considerations for our model.

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:19.700
And we've already done
the first one, right?

00:01:19.700 --> 00:01:22.200
We know we want to
develop this triage system

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:23.910
for chest x-rays.

00:01:23.910 --> 00:01:26.460
And so let's try to do
the rest of these steps.

00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:29.370
We're going to take the three
largest chest x-ray data sets

00:01:29.370 --> 00:01:33.630
that's over 700,000 images
of chest x-rays as our data.

00:01:33.630 --> 00:01:38.400
We're going to create
a finding of no finding

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:39.630
for our label, right?

00:01:39.630 --> 00:01:41.730
So that means if there's a
bunch of different things

00:01:41.730 --> 00:01:43.680
that you could have diagnosed
from the chest x-ray,

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:46.170
and I want to predict if you're
healthy and you can go home,

00:01:46.170 --> 00:01:48.570
the label I should use is no finding.

00:01:48.570 --> 00:01:50.010
I'm going to train a DenseNet,

00:01:50.010 --> 00:01:51.990
which is a kind of
convolutional neural network.

00:01:51.990 --> 00:01:54.300
That's the algorithm
I'm going to optimize.

00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:55.740
And then once I'm done,

00:01:55.740 --> 00:01:57.840
I'm going to look at
my performance, right?

00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:00.180
And so we find in this paper
that we're able to create

00:02:00.180 --> 00:02:03.300
state-of-the-art performance
on this specific task.

00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:04.133
This is great.

00:02:04.133 --> 00:02:07.050
We've gone through this
development pipeline,

00:02:07.050 --> 00:02:08.850
but it's not just us who have gone

00:02:08.850 --> 00:02:10.320
through this development pipeline.

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:11.940
This paper is a few years old now,

00:02:11.940 --> 00:02:15.540
but it was identifying that
there are now a range of tasks

00:02:15.540 --> 00:02:18.390
that AI can do at or above humans

00:02:18.390 --> 00:02:22.110
across different spans
of human tasks, right?

00:02:22.110 --> 00:02:25.980
So anything from tasks that
are done on very young to

00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:27.060
very old people,

00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:30.150
we now have AI that performs
really, really well.

00:02:30.150 --> 00:02:32.490
And so we've achieved
that sort of check mark

00:02:32.490 --> 00:02:34.563
of it has very high performance.

00:02:36.300 --> 00:02:38.880
What if we got through all of these steps.

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:41.880
like I just told you
with the model we have,

00:02:41.880 --> 00:02:43.500
and now we want to consider deploying it

00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:45.480
in the same emergency room?

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:47.520
Well that's okay too.

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:49.680
So in the U.S. we have the
FDA and there's different

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:52.440
regulatory bodies in Canada and in Europe.

00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:54.600
And what happens is
these regulatory agencies

00:02:54.600 --> 00:02:57.600
essentially look at your model
and they audit it the way

00:02:57.600 --> 00:02:59.880
that you would do for a drug, for example,

00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:02.940
looking at its performance
in different settings

00:03:02.940 --> 00:03:05.100
and then they clear it to be released.

00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:08.220
This is a website that
tracks every AI system

00:03:08.220 --> 00:03:11.643
that's been released by the
FDA, approved by the FDA.

00:03:12.660 --> 00:03:14.790
And so you can see that
there are hundreds of these

00:03:14.790 --> 00:03:16.470
across different kinds of settings

00:03:16.470 --> 00:03:18.960
that have already been
approved for deployment.

00:03:18.960 --> 00:03:22.320
So now the question is, let's
go back to my case study.

00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:24.030
So she's having trouble breathing.

00:03:24.030 --> 00:03:26.737
She has this chest x-ray
and they offer her,

00:03:26.737 --> 00:03:30.360
"You can wait two hours for
a chest x-ray to be reviewed

00:03:30.360 --> 00:03:34.470
by clinician or we could have
AI triage you right now."

00:03:34.470 --> 00:03:37.530
So the question is, what
should Sumana choose?

00:03:37.530 --> 00:03:38.700
What would you choose?

00:03:38.700 --> 00:03:40.830
So hands up if you would
choose to be triaged

00:03:40.830 --> 00:03:43.023
by the AI right now, no waiting time.

00:03:44.550 --> 00:03:45.810
Okay, some people.

00:03:45.810 --> 00:03:48.993
Hands up if you would choose
the doctor for two hours.

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:52.590
More people, skeptical crowd.
(laughing)

00:03:52.590 --> 00:03:55.473
Wow, is this a summer school for AI?

00:03:58.079 --> 00:03:59.820
I'm in the wrong room.

00:03:59.820 --> 00:04:03.450
So maybe a reason you wouldn't
choose the AI is you've

00:04:03.450 --> 00:04:06.960
had a bad experience in the
medical system recently.

00:04:06.960 --> 00:04:08.190
So I live in Massachusetts.

00:04:08.190 --> 00:04:10.830
There was a paper that came out
in the "New England Journal"

00:04:10.830 --> 00:04:14.250
this past month actually,
looking at the admissions

00:04:14.250 --> 00:04:17.460
that have happened at 11
Massachusetts hospitals.

00:04:17.460 --> 00:04:20.670
They found that at least
one adverse event occurred

00:04:20.670 --> 00:04:25.670
in over 20% of admissions
due to the provider's error.

00:04:25.770 --> 00:04:29.760
So a doctor, a clinical
staff member made a mistake.

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:33.780
Of those more than 20% were
judged to be preventable

00:04:33.780 --> 00:04:37.500
and over 30% had a severity
level of serious or higher

00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:40.350
that's just in one state
with some random samples

00:04:40.350 --> 00:04:42.840
and looking over this past year.

00:04:42.840 --> 00:04:45.600
But what that means is the federal agency

00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:48.330
in the United States,
ARHQ that's responsible

00:04:48.330 --> 00:04:49.980
for looking at these kinds of mistakes

00:04:49.980 --> 00:04:52.740
and their impact on Americans estimates

00:04:52.740 --> 00:04:54.420
that there are 7.4 million

00:04:54.420 --> 00:04:55.890
U.S. emergency department patients

00:04:55.890 --> 00:04:58.680
that are misdiagnosed and 2.6 million

00:04:58.680 --> 00:05:00.813
that suffer an adverse event as a result.

00:05:01.980 --> 00:05:03.720
And so the question is

00:05:03.720 --> 00:05:08.490
that's the data we're
using to train this model,

00:05:08.490 --> 00:05:10.350
where else would we get it from?

00:05:10.350 --> 00:05:11.610
How well do we think this model

00:05:11.610 --> 00:05:14.190
will perform on a patient like Sumana?

00:05:14.190 --> 00:05:16.230
Do we think it makes similar mistakes

00:05:16.230 --> 00:05:17.403
on all kinds of people?

00:05:18.690 --> 00:05:20.850
And you might say there
are other sources of data

00:05:20.850 --> 00:05:23.130
that we could use that
would maybe supplement this

00:05:23.130 --> 00:05:25.170
so that we don't make serious mistakes.

00:05:25.170 --> 00:05:27.840
For example, we have
randomized controlled trials,

00:05:27.840 --> 00:05:30.150
but randomized control trials
are actually very rare.

00:05:30.150 --> 00:05:31.530
I don't know if you knew this,

00:05:31.530 --> 00:05:33.240
but very few treatments are based on them

00:05:33.240 --> 00:05:36.690
because they're expensive
and they're hard to do.

00:05:36.690 --> 00:05:38.370
One of the reasons they're hard to do is

00:05:38.370 --> 00:05:40.740
because you have to create a population

00:05:40.740 --> 00:05:42.630
where you can only study the effect

00:05:42.630 --> 00:05:44.910
of a specific intervention on an outcome.

00:05:44.910 --> 00:05:46.740
And that means that your
recruited population

00:05:46.740 --> 00:05:49.650
is necessarily extremely narrow.

00:05:49.650 --> 00:05:52.440
And so my favorite statistic
is 94% of asthmatics

00:05:52.440 --> 00:05:55.020
would've been ineligible
for the treatment RCT

00:05:55.020 --> 00:05:56.993
that was used to design their treatment.

00:05:57.840 --> 00:06:00.150
Even worse, what'll
happen sometimes is there

00:06:00.150 --> 00:06:01.860
will be a medical reversal.

00:06:01.860 --> 00:06:05.197
So top journals in medicine
like "Lancet", "JAMA",

00:06:05.197 --> 00:06:06.300
"New England Journal",

00:06:06.300 --> 00:06:07.770
we'll publish a paper saying the thing

00:06:07.770 --> 00:06:09.900
that we thought was good medical practice.

00:06:09.900 --> 00:06:12.600
Actually that's bad and we
should not do that anymore.

00:06:14.280 --> 00:06:16.680
What if instead we look at this

00:06:16.680 --> 00:06:19.050
from the provider's
practice point of view.

00:06:19.050 --> 00:06:21.420
So what happens when you
actually go to the doctor?

00:06:21.420 --> 00:06:23.250
If there's not a randomized control trial

00:06:23.250 --> 00:06:25.770
on your specific condition
and specific drugs,

00:06:25.770 --> 00:06:27.960
they probably are going
to look at what has worked

00:06:27.960 --> 00:06:30.180
for other people like
you in the past, right?

00:06:30.180 --> 00:06:32.520
So doctors will think,
"I know that patients

00:06:32.520 --> 00:06:35.280
have generally reacted
to this medication well."

00:06:35.280 --> 00:06:37.440
So what would happen if we took a dataset

00:06:37.440 --> 00:06:41.250
of 250 million patients and
then looked at the treatments

00:06:41.250 --> 00:06:43.800
they got, just the
medications and then tried

00:06:43.800 --> 00:06:46.080
to identify how many patients

00:06:46.080 --> 00:06:49.020
followed a completely
unique treatment pathway.

00:06:49.020 --> 00:06:52.260
That means they have zero nearest
neighbors in this dataset.

00:06:52.260 --> 00:06:54.960
For common conditions
like diabetes, depression,

00:06:54.960 --> 00:06:56.190
and hypertension.

00:06:56.190 --> 00:06:59.460
For diabetes, it's 10%,
for depression it's 11%,

00:06:59.460 --> 00:07:02.370
and almost a quarter of
hypertensive patients.

00:07:02.370 --> 00:07:03.300
If you ask the question,

00:07:03.300 --> 00:07:05.775
what treatment should
you get next according

00:07:05.775 --> 00:07:06.608
to your nearest neighbor?

00:07:06.608 --> 00:07:07.441
The answer would be, I don't know.

00:07:07.441 --> 00:07:08.400
Because there are zero nearest neighbors

00:07:08.400 --> 00:07:10.653
in this data set of 250 million people.

00:07:12.720 --> 00:07:15.030
In order to move forward
with AI in health,

00:07:15.030 --> 00:07:16.680
we need to think really carefully

00:07:16.680 --> 00:07:19.290
about how these kinds of factors are going

00:07:19.290 --> 00:07:22.110
to affect the model that we create.

00:07:22.110 --> 00:07:24.720
So let's go back and look at this model

00:07:24.720 --> 00:07:26.430
that we trained, that
we were very proud of,

00:07:26.430 --> 00:07:28.080
that we have a paper in "Nature Medicine"

00:07:28.080 --> 00:07:30.660
about just like all those other papers.

00:07:30.660 --> 00:07:34.110
Let's look at the false
positive rate of this model,

00:07:34.110 --> 00:07:38.010
false positives, because if
you falsely positively predict

00:07:38.010 --> 00:07:41.070
that somebody is healthy,
that's underdiagnosis.

00:07:41.070 --> 00:07:43.200
And let's compare that in
different subpopulations

00:07:43.200 --> 00:07:45.030
because if you falsely positively say

00:07:45.030 --> 00:07:47.550
that more women are healthy
when they're actually sick,

00:07:47.550 --> 00:07:48.900
that would lead to underdiagnosis

00:07:48.900 --> 00:07:50.910
in female patients for example.

00:07:50.910 --> 00:07:53.370
And when we run this audit
on our state-of-the-art model

00:07:53.370 --> 00:07:55.770
that you know looks great on average

00:07:55.770 --> 00:07:58.050
and could be cleared by the FDA,

00:07:58.050 --> 00:08:00.360
we find that it has the
largest underdiagnosis rate

00:08:00.360 --> 00:08:02.700
in female patients, young
patients, black patients,

00:08:02.700 --> 00:08:04.890
and patients on public insurance.

00:08:04.890 --> 00:08:06.900
And your underperformance

00:08:06.900 --> 00:08:09.210
is actually worse in
intersectional identity.

00:08:09.210 --> 00:08:11.400
So that means if you're a black
or Hispanic female patient

00:08:11.400 --> 00:08:13.830
in the United States who have
a higher underdiagnosis rate

00:08:13.830 --> 00:08:17.460
than white female patients and
than your aggregated group of

00:08:17.460 --> 00:08:18.510
just female patients.

00:08:19.890 --> 00:08:22.620
This is not just true
in imaging data, right?

00:08:22.620 --> 00:08:23.687
So I used to have to,

00:08:23.687 --> 00:08:25.417
when I was talking about this paper, say,

00:08:25.417 --> 00:08:26.520
"Oh, there are these things

00:08:26.520 --> 00:08:27.960
called contextual language models."

00:08:27.960 --> 00:08:29.820
And I don't have to do that anymore.

00:08:29.820 --> 00:08:31.800
All I have to do is
say, "You all have heard

00:08:31.800 --> 00:08:34.050
of language models, right?"

00:08:34.050 --> 00:08:36.660
So if we do clinical note completion,

00:08:36.660 --> 00:08:39.300
which is a task that actually Epic,

00:08:39.300 --> 00:08:41.970
which is a big EHR provider
in the United States

00:08:41.970 --> 00:08:46.970
and Canada now has in
many hospital systems,

00:08:47.430 --> 00:08:51.120
they've integrated the GPT4
add-on in some hospitals

00:08:51.120 --> 00:08:53.130
where if you're typing a note in Epic,

00:08:53.130 --> 00:08:55.320
you can use the GPT4 plugin to complete

00:08:55.320 --> 00:08:56.760
a patient's note.

00:08:56.760 --> 00:08:58.020
Super helpful, right?

00:08:58.020 --> 00:08:59.970
Taking notes takes a long time.

00:08:59.970 --> 00:09:03.210
So if I start this sentence
from a real patient's note

00:09:03.210 --> 00:09:06.090
from a Boston area hospital
with Caucasian or white patient

00:09:06.090 --> 00:09:07.770
became belligerent and violent,

00:09:07.770 --> 00:09:09.120
SciBERT completes this note

00:09:09.120 --> 00:09:10.950
with "They were sent to hospital".

00:09:10.950 --> 00:09:12.180
If I started with African,

00:09:12.180 --> 00:09:15.248
African-American or black
patient became belligerent

00:09:15.248 --> 00:09:16.081
and violent SciBert completes this

00:09:16.081 --> 00:09:18.150
with "They were sent to prison."

00:09:18.150 --> 00:09:20.820
So this is not just a bias
that's found in imaging data

00:09:20.820 --> 00:09:23.730
and it's not a bias
that is easily removed,

00:09:23.730 --> 00:09:25.410
it's something that is very deeply seeded

00:09:25.410 --> 00:09:26.460
because guess what?

00:09:26.460 --> 00:09:27.690
Humans are biased.

00:09:27.690 --> 00:09:30.690
And so the way that we provide
care is sometimes explicitly

00:09:30.690 --> 00:09:32.250
and implicitly biased.

00:09:32.250 --> 00:09:35.193
And that shows up in the
data that we are using, okay?

00:09:37.170 --> 00:09:39.240
So this is not something
that we can do, you know,

00:09:39.240 --> 00:09:40.950
simply it's an ongoing process

00:09:40.950 --> 00:09:43.653
that's going to require
diverse data and teams.

00:09:44.550 --> 00:09:47.010
I'm going to go through
some parts of this pipeline,

00:09:47.010 --> 00:09:50.040
not all of them because
that would take too long.

00:09:50.040 --> 00:09:52.620
And the first one I want
to talk about is data.

00:09:52.620 --> 00:09:54.270
And I want to talk about data specifically

00:09:54.270 --> 00:09:55.440
in those two modalities,

00:09:55.440 --> 00:09:58.050
notes and imaging because
you might be wondering, okay,

00:09:58.050 --> 00:10:00.423
there are these biases, how bad can it be?

00:10:02.010 --> 00:10:04.590
This is a real note where I've redacted

00:10:04.590 --> 00:10:06.630
the patient's self-reported race.

00:10:06.630 --> 00:10:09.300
Can you tell the race of this patient?

00:10:09.300 --> 00:10:11.370
Probably not because we tested doctors.

00:10:11.370 --> 00:10:13.260
Let me tell you, I have a bunch of friends

00:10:13.260 --> 00:10:14.400
from when I was a graduate student

00:10:14.400 --> 00:10:15.810
that are all doctors and they all hate me

00:10:15.810 --> 00:10:17.280
because I test them all the time

00:10:17.280 --> 00:10:19.410
and they always fail my tests,

00:10:19.410 --> 00:10:21.720
which they're not used to because
they're very good doctors,

00:10:21.720 --> 00:10:24.180
but doctors cannot tell,

00:10:24.180 --> 00:10:26.730
I promise you, whether
this patient self-reports

00:10:26.730 --> 00:10:28.998
their race as black or white.

00:10:28.998 --> 00:10:29.831
Well why should they?

00:10:29.831 --> 00:10:32.400
Well models can and models
can in some cases because

00:10:32.400 --> 00:10:34.890
they're using things that
maybe would consider fair.

00:10:34.890 --> 00:10:37.710
They're using statistical
biases, not societal biases.

00:10:37.710 --> 00:10:39.330
So for example, in the Northeast,

00:10:39.330 --> 00:10:42.120
African-American patients have
a higher rate of hypertension

00:10:42.120 --> 00:10:43.860
and a higher rate of using dialysis.

00:10:43.860 --> 00:10:44.853
Okay, that's fine.

00:10:45.780 --> 00:10:48.270
But it's not just things like that.

00:10:48.270 --> 00:10:50.730
So we looked at two cohorts
in Boston and New York

00:10:50.730 --> 00:10:52.863
and both very simple and
higher capacity models

00:10:52.863 --> 00:10:56.760
that are able to predict
your self-reported race

00:10:56.760 --> 00:10:58.560
with very, very high performance.

00:10:58.560 --> 00:11:00.120
And sometimes they're using things

00:11:00.120 --> 00:11:02.640
that we probably don't want
them to be able to use.

00:11:02.640 --> 00:11:05.970
Like if you call a patient
or their family difficult,

00:11:05.970 --> 00:11:08.820
that patient is probably black, not white.

00:11:08.820 --> 00:11:12.960
If you mention anything
about the skin presented

00:11:12.960 --> 00:11:17.370
illness of a patient,
they are probably white.

00:11:17.370 --> 00:11:18.930
And that's because dermatology textbooks

00:11:18.930 --> 00:11:21.240
actually have almost
no examples of the skin

00:11:21.240 --> 00:11:24.330
related presentation of
illness at all on darker skin.

00:11:24.330 --> 00:11:26.580
So doctors don't know how to diagnose it,

00:11:26.580 --> 00:11:27.540
they don't know how to notice it,

00:11:27.540 --> 00:11:29.504
they don't know how to use it at all.

00:11:29.504 --> 00:11:31.080
So they just don't talk about your skin

00:11:31.080 --> 00:11:32.280
if you have darker skin.

00:11:33.930 --> 00:11:35.550
What about in images?

00:11:35.550 --> 00:11:38.610
We can all imagine how our
implicit or explicit biases

00:11:38.610 --> 00:11:41.463
might show up in a medical
note, right, as we're typing.

00:11:42.330 --> 00:11:44.010
But this is a medical image, right?

00:11:44.010 --> 00:11:45.630
It's a chest x-ray.

00:11:45.630 --> 00:11:47.400
Surely there's nothing here.

00:11:47.400 --> 00:11:49.020
And so also I have a
bunch of radiologists,

00:11:49.020 --> 00:11:50.420
friends who all hate me now.

00:11:51.480 --> 00:11:55.200
Radiologists cannot
tell from a chest x-ray

00:11:55.200 --> 00:11:57.120
whether your self-reported race,

00:11:57.120 --> 00:12:00.510
not your genetic ancestry as
one weird eugenics reviewer

00:12:00.510 --> 00:12:05.190
suggested, your self-reported
race is black or white.

00:12:05.190 --> 00:12:07.053
But machine learning models can,

00:12:07.920 --> 00:12:09.840
they can do so with very,
very high performance.

00:12:09.840 --> 00:12:11.310
They can do so on multiple sites,

00:12:11.310 --> 00:12:12.960
they can do so even when we have

00:12:12.960 --> 00:12:14.820
evenly distributed data sets.

00:12:14.820 --> 00:12:17.100
So we looked at Emory
University where they have

00:12:17.100 --> 00:12:19.260
an even distribution of
black and white patients

00:12:19.260 --> 00:12:22.380
and they can do so across
a variety of modalities.

00:12:22.380 --> 00:12:25.110
And if I ask the radiologist, okay,

00:12:25.110 --> 00:12:28.170
give me a list of every
single spurious correlation

00:12:28.170 --> 00:12:30.600
or proxy or cheating, you know,

00:12:30.600 --> 00:12:32.550
way that you think a neural network

00:12:32.550 --> 00:12:36.210
could be sort of guessing at
somebody's self-reported race.

00:12:36.210 --> 00:12:37.050
And I'll test it.

00:12:37.050 --> 00:12:38.820
It's not any of the ones we tested.

00:12:38.820 --> 00:12:40.950
It's not body mass index, breast density,

00:12:40.950 --> 00:12:43.410
bone density, or disease distribution,

00:12:43.410 --> 00:12:45.000
which was the one I thought it would be.

00:12:45.000 --> 00:12:48.210
In fact, you can filter these
images if you're an EE person,

00:12:48.210 --> 00:12:49.800
you can ban cast filter to these images

00:12:49.800 --> 00:12:52.860
until they do not look
like chest x-rays anymore.

00:12:52.860 --> 00:12:55.530
But even things that
have a little more than,

00:12:55.530 --> 00:12:57.690
you know what we perceive
as spectral noise

00:12:57.690 --> 00:13:00.090
have extremely high performance.

00:13:00.090 --> 00:13:03.270
And so this is not information
that you can easily remove

00:13:03.270 --> 00:13:04.590
from somebody's medical record.

00:13:04.590 --> 00:13:06.450
If you were looking at somebody's notes,

00:13:06.450 --> 00:13:09.187
if you were looking at their
medical images and you thought,

00:13:09.187 --> 00:13:13.020
"Oh well I've removed any
mentions of self-reported race

00:13:13.020 --> 00:13:14.040
from their EHR,

00:13:14.040 --> 00:13:18.390
and so my model won't be able
to do the bias performance

00:13:18.390 --> 00:13:20.310
that you showed."

00:13:20.310 --> 00:13:23.100
It's very deeply proxied
in medical datas in way

00:13:23.100 --> 00:13:25.293
that we cannot detect.

00:13:26.880 --> 00:13:29.160
And so it's important if you
decide to work with health data

00:13:29.160 --> 00:13:33.822
that you recognize, both that
data is a very powerful good,

00:13:33.822 --> 00:13:35.190
it's something that we need to curate.

00:13:35.190 --> 00:13:38.700
It's something that we need to
have available as researchers

00:13:38.700 --> 00:13:41.280
because if we don't have diverse
data sets for research use,

00:13:41.280 --> 00:13:43.470
what will happen is what's
happening right now.

00:13:43.470 --> 00:13:48.470
Namely hospitals will
sell data to companies,

00:13:48.930 --> 00:13:51.390
companies will create
models like I showed you,

00:13:51.390 --> 00:13:53.940
they will be biased and
we in academic spaces

00:13:53.940 --> 00:13:55.980
will not have the capacity to audit them

00:13:55.980 --> 00:13:58.530
because we don't have access
to the same data sets.

00:13:59.610 --> 00:14:01.530
And right now that is what's happening.

00:14:01.530 --> 00:14:03.960
So health really lags other
machine learning subfields

00:14:03.960 --> 00:14:05.580
and reproducibility as it stands.

00:14:05.580 --> 00:14:09.000
And it's largely because
people do not release

00:14:09.000 --> 00:14:11.940
their de-identified data
under data usage agreement.

00:14:11.940 --> 00:14:13.950
There are some data sets
that the community uses,

00:14:13.950 --> 00:14:17.190
but it's really lacking in
terms of what other communities

00:14:17.190 --> 00:14:18.240
have been able to do.

00:14:20.190 --> 00:14:22.080
All right, that's the first section,

00:14:22.080 --> 00:14:24.000
that's what happens with data.

00:14:24.000 --> 00:14:25.380
What about outcome definition?

00:14:25.380 --> 00:14:27.720
Surely there's nothing
sketchy going on here.

00:14:27.720 --> 00:14:29.343
Any questions before I move on?

00:14:36.180 --> 00:14:38.280
So the question was do we
know what was happening

00:14:38.280 --> 00:14:39.113
with the imaging data?

00:14:39.113 --> 00:14:40.350
Is that correct?

00:14:40.350 --> 00:14:42.030
There is no way to prove it,

00:14:42.030 --> 00:14:44.250
but some of our initial guesses are

00:14:44.250 --> 00:14:46.260
that it's spectral
information from bounce back

00:14:46.260 --> 00:14:49.893
from x-rays when you
have more melanin, right?

00:14:51.450 --> 00:14:54.030
Waves actually interact with melanin.

00:14:54.030 --> 00:14:56.010
And so it's possible that that's it.

00:14:56.010 --> 00:14:58.560
We can't prove that, we don't
have photos of people's skin.

00:14:58.560 --> 00:15:00.120
We're assuming that there's correlation

00:15:00.120 --> 00:15:01.173
between these things.

00:15:16.861 --> 00:15:20.444
(audience member mumbling)

00:15:24.510 --> 00:15:27.240
I think it's the differential performance

00:15:27.240 --> 00:15:28.470
that's bias, right?

00:15:28.470 --> 00:15:31.470
So I think there's no
problem in a perfect society

00:15:31.470 --> 00:15:33.780
with a machine learning
model being able to detect

00:15:33.780 --> 00:15:36.600
your self-reported race, who cares, right?

00:15:36.600 --> 00:15:38.940
The fact that a model
could perfectly detect it

00:15:38.940 --> 00:15:41.160
and then it could have some bias judgment

00:15:41.160 --> 00:15:43.110
that is made as a result of it

00:15:43.110 --> 00:15:44.940
because it's learning from other data

00:15:44.940 --> 00:15:47.010
that we treat black patients this way,

00:15:47.010 --> 00:15:49.650
we treat white patients
this way, that's the issue.

00:15:49.650 --> 00:15:53.340
It's not a problem independently
that you could infer this.

00:15:53.340 --> 00:15:54.813
I would say. Yes?

00:16:15.570 --> 00:16:19.080
So what we looked at is whether
within a specific subgroup

00:16:19.080 --> 00:16:23.340
there was a higher or lower
level of false positives

00:16:23.340 --> 00:16:24.720
within the labels.

00:16:24.720 --> 00:16:28.200
So you're right that we are looking

00:16:28.200 --> 00:16:31.950
at the ultimate diagnosis in
the medical records themselves

00:16:31.950 --> 00:16:36.950
as the true label and as
we'll go into in this section,

00:16:37.200 --> 00:16:40.080
maybe there are some reasons
to be skeptical about that,

00:16:40.080 --> 00:16:43.380
but unfortunately that's
a lot of what we have

00:16:43.380 --> 00:16:46.470
to rely on right now unless...

00:16:46.470 --> 00:16:49.440
Some of the dataset that
we used in that study,

00:16:49.440 --> 00:16:51.900
they're not just looking
at the underlying label.

00:16:51.900 --> 00:16:54.810
So I think checks for certainly,

00:16:54.810 --> 00:16:57.750
but also the NIH dataset
had independent radiologists

00:16:57.750 --> 00:17:02.670
review the charts post
hoc and they had to concur

00:17:02.670 --> 00:17:07.670
on the diagnosis in order to
have the label in the dataset.

00:17:09.000 --> 00:17:11.940
And so all of the labels
that we use in general

00:17:11.940 --> 00:17:14.730
in machine learning have a
specific issue I'll go through.

00:17:14.730 --> 00:17:16.890
But you're right that in
health there's an even further

00:17:16.890 --> 00:17:19.230
issue that you could have two radiologists

00:17:19.230 --> 00:17:21.150
that each have a decade of training

00:17:21.150 --> 00:17:22.500
from the best institution

00:17:22.500 --> 00:17:24.930
and they could look at the
same record and disagree

00:17:24.930 --> 00:17:26.070
about the diagnosis.

00:17:26.070 --> 00:17:28.720
That's fundamental uncertainty
that we can't address.

00:17:29.820 --> 00:17:31.560
So for outcome definition,

00:17:31.560 --> 00:17:34.743
getting into some of these
questions that we've been asked,

00:17:35.790 --> 00:17:40.020
this is how you might
create an outcome label

00:17:40.020 --> 00:17:41.760
for a specific task.

00:17:41.760 --> 00:17:44.820
So my kids' school has a meal policy,

00:17:44.820 --> 00:17:46.740
this is true by the way.

00:17:46.740 --> 00:17:47.790
They have a meal policy

00:17:47.790 --> 00:17:50.970
that prohibits high sugar content meals.

00:17:50.970 --> 00:17:52.470
So if you bring a meal to school,

00:17:52.470 --> 00:17:54.210
it can't have high sugar content.

00:17:54.210 --> 00:17:55.860
So let's say that they want to build

00:17:55.860 --> 00:17:57.750
a machine learning model that is helpful

00:17:57.750 --> 00:17:59.550
where you can take a picture of your meal

00:17:59.550 --> 00:18:00.873
and it'll classify like,

00:18:02.820 --> 00:18:05.280
is this meal prohibited
under the school policy?

00:18:05.280 --> 00:18:06.990
So how would we build this model?

00:18:06.990 --> 00:18:08.760
We would take images like this one

00:18:08.760 --> 00:18:10.890
of a breakfast I would enjoy eating

00:18:10.890 --> 00:18:13.710
because I think it's cocoa
puffs and whipped cream.

00:18:13.710 --> 00:18:16.620
And we would ask a bunch of labelers,

00:18:16.620 --> 00:18:18.330
does this have high sugar content?

00:18:18.330 --> 00:18:19.950
Yes or no?

00:18:19.950 --> 00:18:21.930
And then we would train
a machine learning model

00:18:21.930 --> 00:18:23.400
to predict the majority class, right?

00:18:23.400 --> 00:18:25.200
If most people say this
has high sugar content,

00:18:25.200 --> 00:18:27.750
then we should predict it
has high sugar content.

00:18:27.750 --> 00:18:29.370
That's sort of a standard way

00:18:29.370 --> 00:18:31.980
that we do this in all
machine learning tasks.

00:18:31.980 --> 00:18:35.280
But the problem is this is
actually a little bit different

00:18:35.280 --> 00:18:37.620
than the task we're applying it to.

00:18:37.620 --> 00:18:39.780
We're not just trying to
detect high sugar content.

00:18:39.780 --> 00:18:41.280
Usually when we collect labels like this,

00:18:41.280 --> 00:18:43.890
we're applying it not
to a descriptive setting

00:18:43.890 --> 00:18:45.060
like describe this image,

00:18:45.060 --> 00:18:47.190
we're applying it to a normative setting.

00:18:47.190 --> 00:18:51.360
Like if this is true, make a
social decision based on it.

00:18:51.360 --> 00:18:52.290
So what would've happened

00:18:52.290 --> 00:18:54.630
if we would've asked people instead,

00:18:54.630 --> 00:18:57.030
does this meal violate
a school meal policy

00:18:57.030 --> 00:18:59.130
that prohibits high sugar content?

00:18:59.130 --> 00:19:01.620
And you might all be saying
those are exactly the same.

00:19:01.620 --> 00:19:05.460
One is the if condition one
is the then condition, right?

00:19:05.460 --> 00:19:07.980
So it's going to be
approximately the same thing.

00:19:07.980 --> 00:19:09.960
But if you ask people the if condition,

00:19:09.960 --> 00:19:12.690
17 out of 20 say this
is a high sugar meal,

00:19:12.690 --> 00:19:16.470
but only two out of 20 say that
it violates a school policy

00:19:16.470 --> 00:19:18.070
that prohibits high sugar meals.

00:19:19.800 --> 00:19:22.380
And this is not just true
in this meal policy example,

00:19:22.380 --> 00:19:24.330
it's true across a number of settings

00:19:24.330 --> 00:19:27.330
where you might collect data descriptively

00:19:27.330 --> 00:19:29.700
and then apply it normatively.

00:19:29.700 --> 00:19:32.490
So for example, a dress
code for an office,

00:19:32.490 --> 00:19:34.320
a meal policy for a school,

00:19:34.320 --> 00:19:36.990
a pet code for a building
or forum guidelines

00:19:36.990 --> 00:19:38.970
about toxic comments, right?

00:19:38.970 --> 00:19:41.190
And so in each of these
settings we did the same thing.

00:19:41.190 --> 00:19:45.270
We collected data about
specific images or text samples,

00:19:45.270 --> 00:19:47.850
both in a normative
and then a descriptive,

00:19:47.850 --> 00:19:50.495
first descriptive then normative setting,

00:19:50.495 --> 00:19:51.328
on different samples of people.

00:19:51.328 --> 00:19:52.860
And then we looked at what
would happen if we trained

00:19:52.860 --> 00:19:55.560
machine learning models as we often do

00:19:55.560 --> 00:19:56.760
with a descriptive data

00:19:56.760 --> 00:19:58.470
and applied it to the normative setting

00:19:58.470 --> 00:20:00.210
and compared that to
what would've happened

00:20:00.210 --> 00:20:01.890
if you would've trained
the machine learning model

00:20:01.890 --> 00:20:04.650
on labels collected from
the normative setting

00:20:04.650 --> 00:20:05.970
in the first place.

00:20:05.970 --> 00:20:08.250
And what we find is that
models train the way

00:20:08.250 --> 00:20:09.720
that we train them by default right now

00:20:09.720 --> 00:20:12.660
in machine learning
tend to be much harsher

00:20:12.660 --> 00:20:15.930
in their judgments than if
we would have trained them

00:20:15.930 --> 00:20:18.990
on the data that simulates
the normative setting

00:20:18.990 --> 00:20:20.670
we were applying them to.

00:20:20.670 --> 00:20:23.220
So when we collect data
about is this high sugar

00:20:23.220 --> 00:20:24.570
and then apply it to,

00:20:24.570 --> 00:20:27.240
does it violate the
policy of no high sugar?

00:20:27.240 --> 00:20:28.830
We have much harsher judgements

00:20:28.830 --> 00:20:30.990
than if we would've
told people the setting

00:20:30.990 --> 00:20:32.790
that we were applying it to.

00:20:32.790 --> 00:20:37.230
And you can compare the
difference, the impact, the gap,

00:20:37.230 --> 00:20:40.170
this error to what would've
happened if you had

00:20:40.170 --> 00:20:42.750
very high, I would say
rates of label noise,

00:20:42.750 --> 00:20:44.700
high rates of data sub sampling

00:20:44.700 --> 00:20:47.220
or just used a much worse model.

00:20:47.220 --> 00:20:50.763
So if you would've used
a better model, SciBERT,

00:20:51.960 --> 00:20:53.700
in contrast to the ALBERT model

00:20:53.700 --> 00:20:58.170
in the toxic comment classification case,

00:20:58.170 --> 00:21:00.210
the impact is a 1% difference.

00:21:00.210 --> 00:21:01.560
If you use the right data,

00:21:01.560 --> 00:21:04.440
the normatively collected
data in the normative setting,

00:21:04.440 --> 00:21:06.087
the impact is 6.5%.

00:21:06.960 --> 00:21:08.943
So this is a very important problem.

00:21:10.800 --> 00:21:13.740
Another thing that we do,
once we've collected data

00:21:13.740 --> 00:21:16.890
and outcomes, is try to
optimize our algorithms in ways

00:21:16.890 --> 00:21:19.140
that are going to increase
overall performance.

00:21:19.140 --> 00:21:21.750
Before I go into some of the
algorithm development work,

00:21:21.750 --> 00:21:24.123
any questions on outcome definition?

00:21:28.500 --> 00:21:31.680
Alright, great, so how
do we optimize models

00:21:31.680 --> 00:21:32.910
for health settings?

00:21:32.910 --> 00:21:37.200
Well, what we could do is
what happens right now,

00:21:37.200 --> 00:21:38.310
a lot of the time,

00:21:38.310 --> 00:21:40.860
which is we have a very
large data set maybe

00:21:40.860 --> 00:21:43.710
that's held privately by a
hospital or a health provider

00:21:43.710 --> 00:21:45.840
and they won't release their data.

00:21:45.840 --> 00:21:48.330
Their data probably has some asymmetry.

00:21:48.330 --> 00:21:51.300
So the dataset I'm thinking
of is the VA dataset

00:21:51.300 --> 00:21:52.140
in the United States.

00:21:52.140 --> 00:21:54.540
It has more male patients
than female patients.

00:21:54.540 --> 00:21:57.180
And they'll train some sort
of upstream embedding model,

00:21:57.180 --> 00:21:58.770
some sort of representation.

00:21:58.770 --> 00:21:59.910
They're not going to release the data,

00:21:59.910 --> 00:22:01.920
so they just release the
representation, right?

00:22:01.920 --> 00:22:03.690
So they have this latent embedding space.

00:22:03.690 --> 00:22:05.760
Now you private hospitals separately

00:22:05.760 --> 00:22:08.250
can take your own, maybe more balanced,

00:22:08.250 --> 00:22:11.250
perfectly fair data, and
train a downstream classifier

00:22:11.250 --> 00:22:13.890
after you project to our
beautiful embedding space

00:22:13.890 --> 00:22:15.900
that we've trained and released for you.

00:22:15.900 --> 00:22:18.330
So we found that the bias in data

00:22:18.330 --> 00:22:22.590
that causes these sort of
upstream asymmetric embeddings

00:22:22.590 --> 00:22:27.270
actually gets propagated
forward into downstream tasks

00:22:27.270 --> 00:22:30.360
even when you rebalance the training data

00:22:30.360 --> 00:22:32.820
for the downstream task
to be perfectly fair.

00:22:32.820 --> 00:22:35.190
So this is a big problem that we deal

00:22:35.190 --> 00:22:37.980
with looking at the
difference between issues

00:22:37.980 --> 00:22:40.080
with the representation that is learned

00:22:40.080 --> 00:22:43.410
versus the classifier that
could be trained on top of that

00:22:43.410 --> 00:22:45.513
with potentially different or better data.

00:22:46.457 --> 00:22:49.440
Motivated by this, one of the
things we've been looking at

00:22:49.440 --> 00:22:52.920
is how do we use group
attributes and prediction?

00:22:52.920 --> 00:22:55.920
So there's a big question
right now in the United States

00:22:55.920 --> 00:22:58.260
in general in the medical
community about how

00:22:58.260 --> 00:23:00.390
and when we should use
group attributes like sex,

00:23:00.390 --> 00:23:03.540
age, and self-reported race
and clinical risk scores.

00:23:03.540 --> 00:23:05.250
So you can imagine the problem

00:23:05.250 --> 00:23:07.200
is if we use group attributes,

00:23:07.200 --> 00:23:10.470
it can result in worse
subgroup performance

00:23:10.470 --> 00:23:13.140
even though it's improving
the overall group performance.

00:23:13.140 --> 00:23:16.350
So in this example, if we train
a logistic regression model

00:23:16.350 --> 00:23:19.350
to predict sleep apnea with sex and age,

00:23:19.350 --> 00:23:22.830
it's worse for younger male
and older female patients.

00:23:22.830 --> 00:23:25.110
And so they might have
preferred for example,

00:23:25.110 --> 00:23:27.390
the less demographically
personalized model

00:23:27.390 --> 00:23:29.733
because it's helping
most people but not them.

00:23:31.290 --> 00:23:33.570
And the reason that this
happens in general is

00:23:33.570 --> 00:23:35.520
because we either have
model misspecification

00:23:35.520 --> 00:23:36.990
where models are unable to capture

00:23:36.990 --> 00:23:39.570
necessary interaction
effects between a feature

00:23:39.570 --> 00:23:41.220
and a group attribute.

00:23:41.220 --> 00:23:42.690
Or during model selection,

00:23:42.690 --> 00:23:45.570
when we're doing
hyperparameter optimization,

00:23:45.570 --> 00:23:48.027
often what we're doing is
optimizing for overall performance

00:23:48.027 --> 00:23:49.950
and that can result in worse performance

00:23:49.950 --> 00:23:51.933
for a very specific subgroup.

00:23:52.890 --> 00:23:54.720
So what should we do in these cases?

00:23:54.720 --> 00:23:57.930
Our argument in this new paper
appearing next week at ICML

00:23:57.930 --> 00:23:59.880
is that we should really just audit

00:23:59.880 --> 00:24:02.880
and resolve issues of fair
use of demographic data

00:24:02.880 --> 00:24:07.590
as we are doing case specific
usage in clinical risk scores.

00:24:07.590 --> 00:24:10.890
So for example here, if I
wanted to predict a model,

00:24:10.890 --> 00:24:13.050
if I wanted to create a
model to predict mortality

00:24:13.050 --> 00:24:15.120
for acute kidney injury patients,

00:24:15.120 --> 00:24:17.100
what I would do first
is look at what happens

00:24:17.100 --> 00:24:19.980
when I have a group blind classifier, H0,

00:24:19.980 --> 00:24:21.990
look at the performance across groups,

00:24:21.990 --> 00:24:24.930
compare that to a decoupled per group,

00:24:24.930 --> 00:24:27.240
sort of more personalized classifier,

00:24:27.240 --> 00:24:28.620
and then compare that to what happens

00:24:28.620 --> 00:24:32.130
when I use race as an
input predictor in addition

00:24:32.130 --> 00:24:33.990
to other demographic features.

00:24:33.990 --> 00:24:37.260
And here we find that this
leads to worse performance

00:24:37.260 --> 00:24:40.380
in the model across all
metrics for black men.

00:24:40.380 --> 00:24:41.760
And so we would not recommend

00:24:41.760 --> 00:24:44.520
that this is used in this
specific clinical risk score.

00:24:44.520 --> 00:24:46.500
This is one example.

00:24:46.500 --> 00:24:48.420
We show many examples in the paper

00:24:48.420 --> 00:24:51.090
where this is not true, right?

00:24:51.090 --> 00:24:54.060
So it depends specifically
on the data you have

00:24:54.060 --> 00:24:55.710
and the outcome you're trying to predict.

00:24:55.710 --> 00:24:58.920
No one solution resolves
all fair use violations.

00:24:58.920 --> 00:25:01.770
And so this is something you
have to run on a case basis.

00:25:02.940 --> 00:25:04.500
Another thing that might be important

00:25:04.500 --> 00:25:06.690
if you don't want to develop
a clinical risk score

00:25:06.690 --> 00:25:09.840
specifically because you
don't want sort of these

00:25:09.840 --> 00:25:13.650
more regression focused
tasks is a clinical checklist

00:25:13.650 --> 00:25:15.330
or decision list.

00:25:15.330 --> 00:25:17.370
This is very, very popular in medicine

00:25:17.370 --> 00:25:19.830
because checklists are easy
to use, easy to deploy,

00:25:19.830 --> 00:25:21.300
and easy to verify.

00:25:21.300 --> 00:25:25.440
Unfortunately these checklists
are created by humans,

00:25:25.440 --> 00:25:27.030
by a consensus of humans.

00:25:27.030 --> 00:25:29.700
And often what happens is you
spend lots and lots of time

00:25:29.700 --> 00:25:30.840
having experts come together

00:25:30.840 --> 00:25:32.610
and try to define these checklists

00:25:32.610 --> 00:25:35.130
and then later a great
paper, like the one below,

00:25:35.130 --> 00:25:37.350
hidden in plain sight
will be written that says,

00:25:37.350 --> 00:25:38.490
you know, "All these checklists we have,

00:25:38.490 --> 00:25:39.900
they actually don't work very well

00:25:39.900 --> 00:25:42.930
for specific subgroups of people."

00:25:42.930 --> 00:25:44.130
And so one of the things

00:25:44.130 --> 00:25:46.140
that we worked on is actually learning

00:25:46.140 --> 00:25:48.210
an optimally predictive checklist directly

00:25:48.210 --> 00:25:50.520
from data using mixed integer programming.

00:25:50.520 --> 00:25:53.070
The nice thing about this is
you get to have constraints

00:25:53.070 --> 00:25:55.860
like a specific number
of items for example,

00:25:55.860 --> 00:25:58.590
but you can also include
fairness restrictions.

00:25:58.590 --> 00:25:59.610
Why is that important?

00:25:59.610 --> 00:26:01.500
Let's look at a case study.

00:26:01.500 --> 00:26:03.900
So let's say you want to predict mortality

00:26:03.900 --> 00:26:06.300
after a specific treatment in the ICU,

00:26:06.300 --> 00:26:08.100
continuous renal replacement therapy.

00:26:08.100 --> 00:26:10.590
It's an end of life treatment,
it's painful and expensive,

00:26:10.590 --> 00:26:11.820
you don't want to give it if somebody

00:26:11.820 --> 00:26:13.830
will die right after it, okay?

00:26:13.830 --> 00:26:15.570
But we also want to ensure fairness

00:26:15.570 --> 00:26:16.920
across intersectional patient groups

00:26:16.920 --> 00:26:18.450
because just like my first example,

00:26:18.450 --> 00:26:22.050
if you falsely positively predict

00:26:22.050 --> 00:26:25.170
that one subgroup will always
die after this treatment,

00:26:25.170 --> 00:26:26.880
it will lead to them not getting care

00:26:26.880 --> 00:26:29.400
that would actually benefit them, okay?

00:26:29.400 --> 00:26:31.080
So when we train the first model

00:26:31.080 --> 00:26:33.030
with no fairness restrictions,

00:26:33.030 --> 00:26:34.950
we tested this on our ICU colleagues,

00:26:34.950 --> 00:26:36.447
they said "That looks great".

00:26:37.526 --> 00:26:40.080
It's not using any positive proxies.

00:26:40.080 --> 00:26:42.360
For example, one proxy that doctors know

00:26:42.360 --> 00:26:47.160
about in medical data is
biological sex at birth.

00:26:47.160 --> 00:26:49.740
So female patients have lower height,

00:26:49.740 --> 00:26:51.217
lower weight on average they said,

00:26:51.217 --> 00:26:53.401
"Oh, it's not using height and weight.

00:26:53.401 --> 00:26:57.510
So it's probably not biased
against those patients."

00:26:57.510 --> 00:27:00.360
There are some lab tests like EGFR

00:27:00.360 --> 00:27:01.830
that have different ranges

00:27:01.830 --> 00:27:03.990
for patients of different
self-reported race.

00:27:03.990 --> 00:27:05.400
It's not using any of those.

00:27:05.400 --> 00:27:07.830
So this should be a fair model, right?

00:27:07.830 --> 00:27:10.110
Even though we didn't put any
fairness constraints in it.

00:27:10.110 --> 00:27:11.550
But this checklist actually

00:27:11.550 --> 00:27:14.130
has a max false positive
rate gap of over 50%

00:27:14.130 --> 00:27:16.560
between white men and black women.

00:27:16.560 --> 00:27:18.810
So you would not have
wanted to deploy this model.

00:27:18.810 --> 00:27:20.490
Where's the proxy?

00:27:20.490 --> 00:27:24.450
I don't actually know and
neither do the doctors.

00:27:24.450 --> 00:27:28.050
And so it's important to
always check your models

00:27:28.050 --> 00:27:31.800
even if they look completely fine, right?

00:27:31.800 --> 00:27:33.390
When we add in fairness constraints,

00:27:33.390 --> 00:27:34.890
we lose some overall performance,

00:27:34.890 --> 00:27:37.920
but we gain back fairness
within a range that is more

00:27:37.920 --> 00:27:40.923
tolerable for a deployed ICU environment.

00:27:42.510 --> 00:27:43.680
The final thing I want to talk

00:27:43.680 --> 00:27:46.020
about is shifts in algorithm development.

00:27:46.020 --> 00:27:48.870
So shifts are a big, and
robustness to shifts are a big

00:27:48.870 --> 00:27:51.210
issue in the machine learning
community in general.

00:27:51.210 --> 00:27:53.130
But they're particularly
important in health

00:27:53.130 --> 00:27:55.770
because many of the
distributionally robust optimization

00:27:55.770 --> 00:27:59.850
methods that are very wonderful,
perform extremely well

00:27:59.850 --> 00:28:04.230
in other data settings,
really fail in health tasks.

00:28:04.230 --> 00:28:06.780
And further, if you try to
use stable learning algorithms

00:28:06.780 --> 00:28:11.040
like differential privacy as
part of your optimization,

00:28:11.040 --> 00:28:14.340
what we found is that
differential privacy by defaults,

00:28:14.340 --> 00:28:17.070
if anybody works in it, noises and clips

00:28:17.070 --> 00:28:20.400
the gradient effect for data
points that are outliers.

00:28:20.400 --> 00:28:22.440
That's how you guarantee
the differential privacy

00:28:22.440 --> 00:28:24.210
of unique data points.

00:28:24.210 --> 00:28:27.780
Who do you think is an
outlier in medical data?

00:28:27.780 --> 00:28:29.160
Minorities? Yes.

00:28:29.160 --> 00:28:31.380
So guess who gets noised and clipped?

00:28:31.380 --> 00:28:33.690
Don't use differential
privacy in health data.

00:28:33.690 --> 00:28:36.480
It's very scary I have to say.

00:28:36.480 --> 00:28:38.340
All right, so what can we do?

00:28:38.340 --> 00:28:40.560
Can we actually learn
under population shift

00:28:40.560 --> 00:28:43.500
and if so, under which
kinds of population shift?

00:28:43.500 --> 00:28:45.240
And so we look at four different kinds,

00:28:45.240 --> 00:28:48.120
various correlations where
attribute and class are hot on

00:28:48.120 --> 00:28:49.950
the diagonal, attribute imbalance

00:28:49.950 --> 00:28:51.960
where you have more of a
specific kind of attribute

00:28:51.960 --> 00:28:55.155
throughout all classes, class imbalance,

00:28:55.155 --> 00:28:56.010
and then also attribute generalization

00:28:56.010 --> 00:28:57.630
where we just don't observe

00:28:57.630 --> 00:29:00.240
any specific attribute class pair

00:29:00.240 --> 00:29:02.673
and we have to try to
generalize to that shift.

00:29:03.960 --> 00:29:06.510
And we're in luck because actually many

00:29:06.510 --> 00:29:08.610
of the default machine
learning data sets out there

00:29:08.610 --> 00:29:10.830
exhibit these different kinds of shifts.

00:29:10.830 --> 00:29:12.510
Class imbalance and attribute imbalance

00:29:12.510 --> 00:29:14.940
have a lot of health data sets in them.

00:29:14.940 --> 00:29:17.160
So this would be the
chest x-ray multi-site,

00:29:17.160 --> 00:29:19.800
the MIMICNotes, the ChXpert dataset,

00:29:19.800 --> 00:29:21.810
the MIMIC-CXR dataset.

00:29:21.810 --> 00:29:23.700
But a lot of the other datasets

00:29:23.700 --> 00:29:27.540
that we use in distributionally
robust optimization,

00:29:27.540 --> 00:29:30.600
paper benchmarking also
exhibit spurious correlation

00:29:30.600 --> 00:29:32.613
and attribute generalization effects.

00:29:33.690 --> 00:29:36.300
And so what we find when
we benchmark the top papers

00:29:36.300 --> 00:29:38.880
in this space on these different tasks is

00:29:38.880 --> 00:29:41.220
that many of the algorithms
do indeed improve

00:29:41.220 --> 00:29:43.260
on spurious correlation
and class imbalance.

00:29:43.260 --> 00:29:45.600
But even really like great models

00:29:45.600 --> 00:29:48.690
that perform extremely
well like DFR and CRT

00:29:48.690 --> 00:29:51.270
in those two settings do not improve

00:29:51.270 --> 00:29:55.023
and imbalance and attribute
generalization specifically.

00:29:56.550 --> 00:30:00.480
This is an issue that we try
to decompose into, again,

00:30:00.480 --> 00:30:03.240
this relative contribution
between representations

00:30:03.240 --> 00:30:06.090
that can be trained and
classifiers that can be trained.

00:30:06.090 --> 00:30:07.920
So if we freeze a representation

00:30:07.920 --> 00:30:10.800
and try to improve a classifier,
we found that it's possible

00:30:10.800 --> 00:30:13.155
that you can improve spurious correlations

00:30:13.155 --> 00:30:13.988
and class imbalance.

00:30:13.988 --> 00:30:15.780
If you improve the representation itself

00:30:15.780 --> 00:30:17.340
you can have some small impact

00:30:17.340 --> 00:30:19.200
to the attribute imbalance task,

00:30:19.200 --> 00:30:21.390
but nothing improves
attribute generalization.

00:30:21.390 --> 00:30:23.190
So this is a very difficult task

00:30:23.190 --> 00:30:25.830
that is present in a lot
of healthcare settings

00:30:25.830 --> 00:30:28.800
and it's something we need
to develop better models for.

00:30:28.800 --> 00:30:31.710
A final thing to note
is almost every single

00:30:31.710 --> 00:30:35.400
one of these papers, they
optimize for worst-group accuracy.

00:30:35.400 --> 00:30:38.880
It's the gold standard for
subpopulation evaluation.

00:30:38.880 --> 00:30:40.740
But do you remember what we've been going

00:30:40.740 --> 00:30:42.390
through in many of these examples?

00:30:42.390 --> 00:30:43.620
But the thing that we care more

00:30:43.620 --> 00:30:46.260
about is, it's this like
false positive, right?

00:30:46.260 --> 00:30:48.690
Like you don't want to
under diagnose people.

00:30:48.690 --> 00:30:50.790
So worst-group accuracy is correlated

00:30:50.790 --> 00:30:52.140
with things like adjusted accuracy,

00:30:52.140 --> 00:30:54.360
but it's actually inversely proportional

00:30:54.360 --> 00:30:56.820
to worst-group precision.

00:30:56.820 --> 00:30:59.190
So when you are training your models

00:30:59.190 --> 00:31:01.410
and evaluating them for subgroups,

00:31:01.410 --> 00:31:03.690
be very careful which metrics you use

00:31:03.690 --> 00:31:08.100
because it might actually
be significantly worse

00:31:08.100 --> 00:31:09.420
for certain populations.

00:31:09.420 --> 00:31:12.000
And so it's possible if
you're doing a triage task,

00:31:12.000 --> 00:31:13.590
precision is actually what you care

00:31:13.590 --> 00:31:14.853
about more than anything.

00:31:16.620 --> 00:31:19.140
So how do we address some of these shifts?

00:31:19.140 --> 00:31:21.660
Let's say that we have a
big source domain hospital

00:31:21.660 --> 00:31:23.850
where we do really well and then we notice

00:31:23.850 --> 00:31:26.400
that when we apply it to
a target domain hospital,

00:31:26.400 --> 00:31:28.980
it actually has a drop in
performance that's too high.

00:31:28.980 --> 00:31:30.270
We don't like that.

00:31:30.270 --> 00:31:32.580
Wouldn't it be nice if
we could just attribute

00:31:32.580 --> 00:31:35.730
these performance drops to specific shifts

00:31:35.730 --> 00:31:36.660
in the distribution?

00:31:36.660 --> 00:31:38.640
Because those are addressable, right?

00:31:38.640 --> 00:31:40.890
So if we were able to decompose this,

00:31:40.890 --> 00:31:41.977
we could say, you know,

00:31:41.977 --> 00:31:44.430
"There's different parts of the source

00:31:44.430 --> 00:31:47.010
and target distribution and
maybe I can collect more data

00:31:47.010 --> 00:31:49.410
in the source or only have it apply

00:31:49.410 --> 00:31:52.200
to specific populations in the target."

00:31:52.200 --> 00:31:55.140
And so we actually have a
new paper where we do this.

00:31:55.140 --> 00:31:57.990
We model shift attribution as a game.

00:31:57.990 --> 00:32:01.110
You assume this is a
reasonably large assumption,

00:32:01.110 --> 00:32:03.420
but you can do it in
some medical settings.

00:32:03.420 --> 00:32:05.640
You have to assume that you
can specify a causal graph

00:32:05.640 --> 00:32:07.470
that is reasonable for your setting.

00:32:07.470 --> 00:32:09.060
And then you can model shift attribution

00:32:09.060 --> 00:32:10.830
as a cooperative game where the players

00:32:10.830 --> 00:32:13.410
are mechanisms of attribution,

00:32:13.410 --> 00:32:15.900
each player contributes
to the target distribution

00:32:15.900 --> 00:32:17.340
when it's in a coalition

00:32:17.340 --> 00:32:20.130
and to a source distribution otherwise.

00:32:20.130 --> 00:32:22.290
And the attribution mechanism is actually

00:32:22.290 --> 00:32:23.730
the Shapley Value.

00:32:23.730 --> 00:32:26.970
And so when we do this and
run it through a case study,

00:32:26.970 --> 00:32:30.030
what we get is, let's say
that we have a algorithm

00:32:30.030 --> 00:32:32.190
that's trained to predict
in hospital mortality

00:32:32.190 --> 00:32:33.810
using vitals and labs from hospitals

00:32:33.810 --> 00:32:36.210
in the American Midwest, large sample.

00:32:36.210 --> 00:32:38.100
And then the model is
deployed in hospitals

00:32:38.100 --> 00:32:40.350
in the American South smaller sample.

00:32:40.350 --> 00:32:42.870
And we noticed that there is
an increase in the Brier score.

00:32:42.870 --> 00:32:44.970
So it's more poorly calibrated

00:32:44.970 --> 00:32:47.100
when we apply it to the target.

00:32:47.100 --> 00:32:48.750
If we want to ask why the model fails,

00:32:48.750 --> 00:32:50.760
we can come up with a causal graph using

00:32:50.760 --> 00:32:52.260
the data features we have.

00:32:52.260 --> 00:32:55.500
We can run our method, we
can say maybe it's going

00:32:55.500 --> 00:32:56.910
to be easiest to focus on

00:32:56.910 --> 00:32:58.860
the difference of distribution in age.

00:32:58.860 --> 00:33:01.230
That's a mechanism of
shift I want to tackle.

00:33:01.230 --> 00:33:03.030
We can look at the
difference between source

00:33:03.030 --> 00:33:04.440
and target distributions of age

00:33:04.440 --> 00:33:07.620
and say "Aha, what we can
do is collect more data

00:33:07.620 --> 00:33:09.630
from older people on the source."

00:33:09.630 --> 00:33:11.760
And we find that when we
do this, when we deploy

00:33:11.760 --> 00:33:16.760
this specific targeted
data collection mechanism,

00:33:16.800 --> 00:33:20.430
we have much better
performance at the target site

00:33:20.430 --> 00:33:21.360
and it's better.

00:33:21.360 --> 00:33:23.670
There is a lower distance between source

00:33:23.670 --> 00:33:27.420
and target distribution
performance than if you

00:33:27.420 --> 00:33:30.270
just did naive retraining with
the source and the target.

00:33:32.190 --> 00:33:34.410
So the final thing I wanted to talk about,

00:33:34.410 --> 00:33:37.350
which is maybe the most
outside of my wheelhouse

00:33:37.350 --> 00:33:39.330
and maybe many of your wheelhouses given

00:33:39.330 --> 00:33:42.000
that this is a machine
learning summer school,

00:33:42.000 --> 00:33:43.650
is post-deployment considerations.

00:33:43.650 --> 00:33:45.930
Because it's possible none of you really

00:33:45.930 --> 00:33:48.000
will work on deployments,

00:33:48.000 --> 00:33:50.400
but it's a thing I think
you should keep in mind

00:33:51.240 --> 00:33:53.790
because even if you
don't deploy your model,

00:33:53.790 --> 00:33:56.310
if your code is on GitHub, somebody might.

00:33:56.310 --> 00:33:58.740
So let's think about
how we can ensure models

00:33:58.740 --> 00:34:01.530
will augment and improve human healthcare.

00:34:01.530 --> 00:34:05.610
So this is the transcript
from a crisis line call.

00:34:05.610 --> 00:34:09.300
This is a reasonably common
thing that people volunteer

00:34:09.300 --> 00:34:12.660
for in the United States
where for example,

00:34:12.660 --> 00:34:14.430
you get some training saying you're

00:34:14.430 --> 00:34:16.050
going to get texts like this.

00:34:16.050 --> 00:34:19.080
I've added the highlighting
for self-reported race

00:34:19.080 --> 00:34:21.750
and also for religious identity.

00:34:21.750 --> 00:34:23.430
Normally this is all just black text

00:34:23.430 --> 00:34:25.470
and that's what our participants got.

00:34:25.470 --> 00:34:27.210
And we had people read through this

00:34:27.210 --> 00:34:28.380
and we gave them some training.

00:34:28.380 --> 00:34:30.960
We said, you know, "You have two options.

00:34:30.960 --> 00:34:33.540
There's no liability risk
because this patient is going

00:34:33.540 --> 00:34:35.220
to get help no matter what,

00:34:35.220 --> 00:34:38.340
but if there's a risk of violence,

00:34:38.340 --> 00:34:39.810
you should call the police, right?"

00:34:39.810 --> 00:34:43.380
So just like in the outcome
definition part of the talk,

00:34:43.380 --> 00:34:45.090
we have an if condition,

00:34:45.090 --> 00:34:48.180
if there's a risk of violence
and a then condition,

00:34:48.180 --> 00:34:50.970
then you should call the police, okay?

00:34:50.970 --> 00:34:53.430
And then we ask people to
make this decision, right?

00:34:53.430 --> 00:34:55.710
So they should always
call for medical help

00:34:55.710 --> 00:34:57.150
unless there's this risk of violence

00:34:57.150 --> 00:34:58.410
and then they should call the police.

00:34:58.410 --> 00:35:02.400
And so at baseline people
just saw this and this task.

00:35:02.400 --> 00:35:06.180
And then we have one
intervention we do where

00:35:06.180 --> 00:35:08.940
we give them some advice and
we either give them advice

00:35:08.940 --> 00:35:12.090
from a biased GPT model that
we intentionally biased.

00:35:12.090 --> 00:35:14.617
It's easy to do so that it always says

00:35:14.617 --> 00:35:17.550
"There's a risk of violence",
it always has Y equals one,

00:35:17.550 --> 00:35:18.383
there's a risk of violence,

00:35:18.383 --> 00:35:20.670
you should call the
police disproportionately

00:35:20.670 --> 00:35:22.920
for black and Muslim subjects.

00:35:22.920 --> 00:35:24.570
And then we had an unbiased model

00:35:24.570 --> 00:35:26.340
where we fixed the probability.

00:35:26.340 --> 00:35:28.740
So it did not disproportionately
say you should call

00:35:28.740 --> 00:35:31.380
the police on black and
Muslim subjects, okay?

00:35:31.380 --> 00:35:35.190
So treatment number one is
whether you as a volunteer

00:35:35.190 --> 00:35:36.330
or if you're not in the control group,

00:35:36.330 --> 00:35:38.970
that gets no advice, treatment
number one is whether you get

00:35:38.970 --> 00:35:42.870
the biased or unbiased GPT
model giving you advice.

00:35:42.870 --> 00:35:46.980
Treatment number two is
whether you get the descriptive

00:35:46.980 --> 00:35:50.430
or the prescriptive part
of the training, right?

00:35:50.430 --> 00:35:52.980
So remember we said if
there's no risk of violence,

00:35:52.980 --> 00:35:54.570
you should call for medical help.

00:35:54.570 --> 00:35:56.340
If and only if there's a risk of violence,

00:35:56.340 --> 00:35:57.240
then you should call the police.

00:35:57.240 --> 00:35:58.590
So in the y equals one case,

00:35:58.590 --> 00:36:01.830
we could either give them the
descriptive recommendation,

00:36:01.830 --> 00:36:03.990
RAI systems flag this
call for risk of violence,

00:36:03.990 --> 00:36:06.120
or we could give them the then condition.

00:36:06.120 --> 00:36:08.130
In this situation, our
model thinks you should call

00:36:08.130 --> 00:36:10.893
for police or medical help, right?

00:36:11.970 --> 00:36:13.590
And so this is the second treatment.

00:36:13.590 --> 00:36:16.320
So there's two things that
are being randomized here.

00:36:16.320 --> 00:36:17.550
If you're in the study group,

00:36:17.550 --> 00:36:19.410
not the control group with no advice,

00:36:19.410 --> 00:36:21.390
you're randomized to either get the biased

00:36:21.390 --> 00:36:25.320
or unbiased GPT model and then
to either get the descriptive

00:36:25.320 --> 00:36:27.933
or prescriptive recommendations, right?

00:36:29.700 --> 00:36:31.620
And then you make the same
decision, you see the same text,

00:36:31.620 --> 00:36:33.090
everything else is the same.

00:36:33.090 --> 00:36:35.310
So just as a show of hands,

00:36:35.310 --> 00:36:37.500
who thinks that the first treatment

00:36:37.500 --> 00:36:39.270
is what's going to matter most?

00:36:39.270 --> 00:36:42.543
Whether we gave you the
biased or unbiased GPT model.

00:36:44.520 --> 00:36:46.140
Nobody thinks that.

00:36:46.140 --> 00:36:47.290
Some people think that.

00:36:48.383 --> 00:36:49.620
Who thinks that treatment number two,

00:36:49.620 --> 00:36:51.000
whether we give you the if

00:36:51.000 --> 00:36:53.940
or then condition is what's
going to matter most?

00:36:53.940 --> 00:36:55.413
A lot of people think that.

00:36:56.490 --> 00:37:00.090
So what we found is that
clinicians, actually

00:37:00.090 --> 00:37:03.270
a really large group of
clinicians and non-clinicians

00:37:03.270 --> 00:37:05.190
are not more likely to call the police

00:37:05.190 --> 00:37:07.170
on black and Muslim subjects at baseline.

00:37:07.170 --> 00:37:08.100
And that's important, right?

00:37:08.100 --> 00:37:09.300
Like when they're given no advice,

00:37:09.300 --> 00:37:10.530
it's they're not just proportionately

00:37:10.530 --> 00:37:13.050
likely to call police on minorities.

00:37:13.050 --> 00:37:15.630
However, when you give
them, as you all guessed,

00:37:15.630 --> 00:37:18.660
the biased prescriptive recommendations,

00:37:18.660 --> 00:37:22.380
clinicians and non-clinicians
are both much more likely

00:37:22.380 --> 00:37:25.110
to call the police on black
and Muslim individuals.

00:37:25.110 --> 00:37:26.370
But shockingly for me,

00:37:26.370 --> 00:37:28.590
but not for you all who
are much more skeptical

00:37:28.590 --> 00:37:29.703
and cynical than I am,

00:37:30.870 --> 00:37:33.690
the descriptive flags
from the biased models

00:37:33.690 --> 00:37:35.550
didn't have the same effect.

00:37:35.550 --> 00:37:36.990
And this allowed the participants

00:37:36.990 --> 00:37:41.990
to have retention of their
regional fair decision making.

00:37:42.210 --> 00:37:44.730
The reason this is shocking to me is

00:37:44.730 --> 00:37:47.370
because this is not part
of certainly my training,

00:37:47.370 --> 00:37:49.950
but I would say globally are training.

00:37:49.950 --> 00:37:52.920
If I tell you to predict a
label, it doesn't matter.

00:37:52.920 --> 00:37:56.040
You've predicted Y is equal
to one, you don't think about,

00:37:56.040 --> 00:37:57.300
well, when you deploy this,

00:37:57.300 --> 00:37:59.460
make sure you have the if condition,

00:37:59.460 --> 00:38:02.220
not the then condition or
the results will be wild.

00:38:02.220 --> 00:38:03.513
Nobody tells you that.

00:38:04.680 --> 00:38:06.190
And I don't think it's a very

00:38:07.140 --> 00:38:12.030
commonly thought of issue in
deployments in machine learning

00:38:12.030 --> 00:38:15.000
that when we are
optimizing for an outcome,

00:38:15.000 --> 00:38:16.920
there's different ways in which the model

00:38:16.920 --> 00:38:18.000
could give its advice.

00:38:18.000 --> 00:38:21.150
And it doesn't matter if you
have exactly the same model,

00:38:21.150 --> 00:38:23.460
tiny differences in how you present

00:38:23.460 --> 00:38:26.310
that advice even to experts in this case,

00:38:26.310 --> 00:38:29.910
will cause them to
follow incorrect prompts.

00:38:29.910 --> 00:38:30.743
Yes?

00:38:39.780 --> 00:38:42.090
Nothing we didn't even tell them it

00:38:42.090 --> 00:38:43.080
was a GPT model.

00:38:43.080 --> 00:38:47.340
We said we are training an
AI system to assist people

00:38:47.340 --> 00:38:49.020
with triaging, right?

00:38:49.020 --> 00:38:51.360
You are going to test this for us.

00:38:51.360 --> 00:38:52.770
There will be some advice,

00:38:52.770 --> 00:38:54.670
you should still follow your training.

00:38:57.810 --> 00:38:59.763
Any other question? Clarification?

00:39:03.030 --> 00:39:07.080
All right, so a final
thing that I want to go

00:39:07.080 --> 00:39:12.080
through is I'm often asked by many people

00:39:13.320 --> 00:39:16.893
whether we can just fix these
issues with explanations.

00:39:18.120 --> 00:39:21.630
Actually I would say
maybe half of the papers

00:39:21.630 --> 00:39:23.460
that I'm presenting here today,

00:39:23.460 --> 00:39:26.760
what happened was I gave a presentation

00:39:26.760 --> 00:39:29.550
and there was this one
person who was so obnoxious

00:39:29.550 --> 00:39:31.740
and just said, "You
can fix that with this,

00:39:31.740 --> 00:39:33.210
you can fix that with this."

00:39:33.210 --> 00:39:35.520
And then I had this like
villain era rage year

00:39:35.520 --> 00:39:37.740
where all my graduate
students just wrote papers

00:39:37.740 --> 00:39:41.137
denying every claim this person made.

00:39:41.137 --> 00:39:42.210
(laughing)

00:39:42.210 --> 00:39:44.430
This is one of those papers.

00:39:44.430 --> 00:39:45.813
So I like it a lot.

00:39:47.400 --> 00:39:52.400
So if anybody ever asks you
can you fix your model bias

00:39:52.830 --> 00:39:55.830
with explanations, the argument is,

00:39:55.830 --> 00:39:57.300
well these models are very complex

00:39:57.300 --> 00:39:59.970
and the complexity is part of the issue.

00:39:59.970 --> 00:40:02.970
So can we use these
post-hoc explanation methods

00:40:02.970 --> 00:40:04.740
in order to make them safer?

00:40:04.740 --> 00:40:09.660
And this is particularly
a common justification

00:40:09.660 --> 00:40:11.550
in health for example,

00:40:11.550 --> 00:40:13.830
for designing a new explanation method.

00:40:13.830 --> 00:40:16.080
Some of these methods
are extremely popular.

00:40:16.080 --> 00:40:20.010
When I checked last LIME
had over 8,000 citations.

00:40:20.010 --> 00:40:22.680
So it's a very common thing that happens.

00:40:22.680 --> 00:40:25.890
You could do local or
global explanation methods,

00:40:25.890 --> 00:40:28.590
but the issue here is that
what is an explanation method?

00:40:28.590 --> 00:40:30.840
An explanation method is fundamentally

00:40:30.840 --> 00:40:33.210
a local or global linear projection often

00:40:33.210 --> 00:40:35.433
into a reduced space, right?

00:40:36.480 --> 00:40:37.620
That's how you get

00:40:37.620 --> 00:40:42.620
this explainability facet of the method.

00:40:42.720 --> 00:40:45.570
And maybe that does make
it easier to interpret,

00:40:45.570 --> 00:40:48.510
but the problem is like
all approximations,

00:40:48.510 --> 00:40:50.910
there's going to be some
reduction in quality.

00:40:50.910 --> 00:40:54.570
So our question is who has
the reduction in quality?

00:40:54.570 --> 00:40:56.340
So we measured the fairness of local

00:40:56.340 --> 00:40:58.860
and global explanation methods
and we're going to compare

00:40:58.860 --> 00:41:01.860
the difference between
average fidelity across groups

00:41:01.860 --> 00:41:03.810
and your worst case fidelity to

00:41:03.810 --> 00:41:05.790
the original model performance

00:41:05.790 --> 00:41:08.250
and then also the average
absolute difference

00:41:08.250 --> 00:41:10.260
in fidelity across subgroups, right?

00:41:10.260 --> 00:41:13.140
So if you have a model and
it performs in certain ways

00:41:13.140 --> 00:41:14.310
and then you explain it,

00:41:14.310 --> 00:41:16.860
how much worse is the
explanation for different people

00:41:16.860 --> 00:41:19.650
than if you just would've
used the original model?

00:41:19.650 --> 00:41:23.280
So we are not going to just
test this in the health MIMIC

00:41:23.280 --> 00:41:25.470
setting of predicting ICU mortality,

00:41:25.470 --> 00:41:27.300
which is usually what I do.

00:41:27.300 --> 00:41:31.560
We also wanted to test this
across different human process

00:41:31.560 --> 00:41:34.710
areas where AI has been
either proposed for

00:41:34.710 --> 00:41:38.580
or is actively used for
assisting decision making.

00:41:38.580 --> 00:41:40.260
So this would be in financial markets

00:41:40.260 --> 00:41:42.030
for adult income prediction, for example,

00:41:42.030 --> 00:41:45.360
to just determine whether you're
a credit worthy for a loan.

00:41:45.360 --> 00:41:47.190
This would be for an educational

00:41:47.190 --> 00:41:50.370
setting predicting whether
students pass specific exams.

00:41:50.370 --> 00:41:54.810
And then in criminal justice
already there are models

00:41:54.810 --> 00:41:59.040
that are allowed to predict
recidivism risk so that judges

00:41:59.040 --> 00:42:02.010
could use it in bail
hearings to determine bail.

00:42:02.010 --> 00:42:03.090
So these are all settings

00:42:03.090 --> 00:42:05.320
where you could have an advice giving

00:42:07.020 --> 00:42:09.750
AI that is deployed within the setting

00:42:09.750 --> 00:42:12.660
and then the person making
the decision gets to use

00:42:12.660 --> 00:42:15.780
that advice and maybe
there's an original model.

00:42:15.780 --> 00:42:18.240
And then there's also
this explained version.

00:42:18.240 --> 00:42:21.030
We find that the
explanation models, right?

00:42:21.030 --> 00:42:24.510
So for both local and global explanations,

00:42:24.510 --> 00:42:26.670
they induce subgroup fidelity gaps,

00:42:26.670 --> 00:42:28.260
which should not be shocking, right?

00:42:28.260 --> 00:42:29.093
Like who do you think

00:42:29.093 --> 00:42:31.593
gets approximated into worse performance?

00:42:32.741 --> 00:42:34.380
And we also find that this gap varies

00:42:34.380 --> 00:42:36.840
with the dimension of data representation

00:42:36.840 --> 00:42:38.340
in the explanation model.

00:42:38.340 --> 00:42:39.600
That also makes sense.

00:42:39.600 --> 00:42:41.790
The more you explain a model, right?

00:42:41.790 --> 00:42:44.280
So like the lower dimensionality
you force it to be,

00:42:44.280 --> 00:42:46.170
the worse the approximation errors get

00:42:46.170 --> 00:42:48.930
and it disproportionately
affects subgroups often

00:42:48.930 --> 00:42:50.880
that are underrepresented in your data.

00:42:52.860 --> 00:42:57.630
A final thing that is maybe
more related to deployments

00:42:57.630 --> 00:43:02.100
than the underlying technology
is people really trust things

00:43:02.100 --> 00:43:06.750
when they're explained
in ways that are, again,

00:43:06.750 --> 00:43:08.820
very surprising to me.

00:43:08.820 --> 00:43:11.700
So humans, there are these
fantastic experiments

00:43:11.700 --> 00:43:15.150
by Dr. Ayanna Howard of
formerly Georgia Tech,

00:43:15.150 --> 00:43:17.400
I don't know if she's still there,

00:43:17.400 --> 00:43:20.310
where she had a bunch of
students in a room like this

00:43:20.310 --> 00:43:24.750
and then smoke started pouring
into the door and you know,

00:43:24.750 --> 00:43:26.700
fire alarms went off and then there was

00:43:26.700 --> 00:43:28.620
this cute little rescue robot

00:43:28.620 --> 00:43:29.970
that they had previously seen

00:43:29.970 --> 00:43:32.280
do a terrible job at navigation,

00:43:32.280 --> 00:43:33.847
come into the room and say, you know,

00:43:33.847 --> 00:43:35.580
"Follow me to safety."

00:43:35.580 --> 00:43:37.770
And then people follow this robot past

00:43:37.770 --> 00:43:41.940
the clearly marked exit that
looks safe towards a room

00:43:41.940 --> 00:43:44.370
that looks like there is no exit

00:43:44.370 --> 00:43:46.670
and the fire might
actually be coming from it.

00:43:48.450 --> 00:43:53.280
Truly wild, so you know, this
has already been established

00:43:53.280 --> 00:43:55.950
in robotic settings.

00:43:55.950 --> 00:43:58.620
Roboticists that look at
human-computer interaction

00:43:58.620 --> 00:44:01.740
have already established
that people can over-rely

00:44:01.740 --> 00:44:04.470
on robotic systems
specifically in two settings

00:44:04.470 --> 00:44:06.870
where they believe a robot
can perform a function

00:44:06.870 --> 00:44:09.720
that it can't, right?

00:44:09.720 --> 00:44:13.200
Or when the person expects
that the system mitigates risk,

00:44:13.200 --> 00:44:14.250
it has some fail safe,

00:44:14.250 --> 00:44:16.890
it's going to mitigate like crazy risks.

00:44:16.890 --> 00:44:19.260
And those are both true
in health, I would argue.

00:44:19.260 --> 00:44:22.770
Another thing that is really fun is

00:44:22.770 --> 00:44:24.570
the fake group at Microsoft Research.

00:44:24.570 --> 00:44:27.273
This is Hanna Wallach, Jenn
Wortman Vaughn and team,

00:44:28.380 --> 00:44:31.380
they did this really fun
experiment where they took people

00:44:31.380 --> 00:44:34.890
in New York where
housing prices, you know,

00:44:34.890 --> 00:44:35.850
fluctuate quite a bit.

00:44:35.850 --> 00:44:37.800
And so most New Yorkers are very aware

00:44:37.800 --> 00:44:40.830
of like what a place would go for, right?

00:44:40.830 --> 00:44:43.170
So they surveyed people about, you know,

00:44:43.170 --> 00:44:46.500
what do you think this
this unit would go for?

00:44:46.500 --> 00:44:48.180
And then they intentionally added,

00:44:48.180 --> 00:44:50.310
and then they had a machine learning model

00:44:50.310 --> 00:44:52.650
give its prediction of
what it would go for.

00:44:52.650 --> 00:44:53.970
They had both a black-box model

00:44:53.970 --> 00:44:55.470
and one that explained its prediction

00:44:55.470 --> 00:44:57.510
by taking bedrooms times something

00:44:57.510 --> 00:44:59.220
plus bathrooms times something,

00:44:59.220 --> 00:45:01.440
and gave you a market prediction.

00:45:01.440 --> 00:45:03.390
And they had some intentionally
misconfigured units.

00:45:03.390 --> 00:45:05.940
So instead of a three bed,
one bath, it was a three bath,

00:45:05.940 --> 00:45:09.900
one bed, which you would imagine
would not go for very much.

00:45:09.900 --> 00:45:12.360
And so people predicted that at baseline.

00:45:12.360 --> 00:45:14.370
When you gave them a
black-box model that predicted

00:45:14.370 --> 00:45:16.650
that this three bathroom one bedroom

00:45:16.650 --> 00:45:17.887
would go for a very high price, they said,

00:45:17.887 --> 00:45:20.760
"Well the model is wrong obviously,

00:45:20.760 --> 00:45:22.500
and we're not fooled by it."

00:45:22.500 --> 00:45:24.180
But when you explained it with a formula,

00:45:24.180 --> 00:45:25.537
well I just took this
times this, they said,

00:45:25.537 --> 00:45:27.840
"Oh no, sounds good."

00:45:27.840 --> 00:45:30.423
Then they took the higher market value.

00:45:31.950 --> 00:45:34.830
And so, you know, we over trusts things

00:45:34.830 --> 00:45:37.200
that we think we can see a mechanism for.

00:45:37.200 --> 00:45:38.700
In general, it's a human thing.

00:45:38.700 --> 00:45:41.460
It's not AI's fault, it's how we behave.

00:45:41.460 --> 00:45:44.790
And the final thing is medicine
has a lot of black-boxes

00:45:44.790 --> 00:45:47.310
that we use because we don't
know exactly how it works.

00:45:47.310 --> 00:45:49.680
We do not know how acetaminophen works.

00:45:49.680 --> 00:45:51.750
Think of about the next
time you take it actually.

00:45:51.750 --> 00:45:52.920
We dunno how lithium works.

00:45:52.920 --> 00:45:55.050
And I guarantee you most
doctors who prescribe an MRI

00:45:55.050 --> 00:45:58.440
do not know exactly how
the MRI machine works.

00:45:58.440 --> 00:45:59.880
We trust that there is a system

00:45:59.880 --> 00:46:02.460
that vetted how these
things should be used.

00:46:02.460 --> 00:46:03.870
We trust that there are audits

00:46:03.870 --> 00:46:06.630
that the MRI machine is
serviced on a regular contract.

00:46:06.630 --> 00:46:10.440
We trust a system of
regulation around it even

00:46:10.440 --> 00:46:13.263
if we don't understand
exactly how a thing works.

00:46:14.400 --> 00:46:17.430
And so if we need to trust
a system of regulation

00:46:17.430 --> 00:46:20.160
around something, how do we get there?

00:46:20.160 --> 00:46:22.200
And the analogy that I'm
going to make here is

00:46:22.200 --> 00:46:26.340
to aviation where it used
to be very unsafe to fly.

00:46:26.340 --> 00:46:27.330
I didn't know this,

00:46:27.330 --> 00:46:30.960
but there's a aviation person
at MIT who was telling me

00:46:30.960 --> 00:46:33.363
the history and I'm very
thankful I live now.

00:46:35.010 --> 00:46:38.280
So aviation in the United
States has a lot of very formal

00:46:38.280 --> 00:46:40.080
feedback loops in the federal government.

00:46:40.080 --> 00:46:42.780
So lots of different agencies
work together to enhance

00:46:42.780 --> 00:46:45.930
reporting, evaluation, and
auditing of different mechanisms.

00:46:45.930 --> 00:46:47.550
There's a culture of safety.

00:46:47.550 --> 00:46:49.770
So there's a federal agency that actually

00:46:49.770 --> 00:46:52.950
has open safety investigations
where they assume

00:46:52.950 --> 00:46:54.420
there's no blame assigned

00:46:54.420 --> 00:46:55.770
until there's a full investigation.

00:46:55.770 --> 00:46:58.560
And then they try to do
fair liability attribution

00:46:58.560 --> 00:47:00.570
and there's a lot of training

00:47:00.570 --> 00:47:03.180
and this is completely
absent in healthcare,

00:47:03.180 --> 00:47:05.790
which is something that I
think is really frightening.

00:47:05.790 --> 00:47:07.680
Pilots undergo really extensive training,

00:47:07.680 --> 00:47:09.630
like thousands of hours in simulations.

00:47:09.630 --> 00:47:12.060
So they know how to interact
with these automated systems

00:47:12.060 --> 00:47:14.280
that give them prompts at different times.

00:47:14.280 --> 00:47:16.170
We need to do the same thing in healthcare

00:47:16.170 --> 00:47:19.260
so we can mitigate some
of these automation biases

00:47:19.260 --> 00:47:21.660
with training that includes tools.

00:47:21.660 --> 00:47:24.030
There's many ways you could think

00:47:24.030 --> 00:47:27.180
about mapping this aviation model into

00:47:27.180 --> 00:47:29.670
at least the American government.

00:47:29.670 --> 00:47:32.880
The FDA on our side could mandate audits.

00:47:32.880 --> 00:47:36.210
We have the FTC and
ARHQ that could monitor

00:47:36.210 --> 00:47:39.780
what's happening within
different parts of deployments.

00:47:39.780 --> 00:47:43.590
There could be ARPA-H that
provides open data banks

00:47:43.590 --> 00:47:45.780
of representative groups.

00:47:45.780 --> 00:47:48.540
So you can train and
audit your own models.

00:47:48.540 --> 00:47:51.090
CMS could do regulation and accreditation.

00:47:51.090 --> 00:47:52.800
You don't get reimbursed by the government

00:47:52.800 --> 00:47:55.380
unless you've been
accredited for performing

00:47:55.380 --> 00:48:00.380
at a certain rate and at least
hypothetically on our side,

00:48:01.350 --> 00:48:02.820
HHS could do enforcement.

00:48:02.820 --> 00:48:05.340
So if we find that your
models are underperforming

00:48:05.340 --> 00:48:06.750
in a deployed setting,

00:48:06.750 --> 00:48:10.230
then we're going to enforce
the legal code against you.

00:48:10.230 --> 00:48:13.440
There are some problems,
hopefully I have convinced you,

00:48:13.440 --> 00:48:16.590
that are unique to health
that we can't just map over.

00:48:16.590 --> 00:48:19.110
And these are things that
are fundamentally inequities

00:48:19.110 --> 00:48:20.850
and underlying data and processes

00:48:20.850 --> 00:48:23.640
and we don't want them to
be learned and automated.

00:48:23.640 --> 00:48:26.250
We were asked by a reviewer,
"What could those possibly be?"

00:48:26.250 --> 00:48:28.620
So here are some examples.

00:48:28.620 --> 00:48:32.520
Yeah, I, you know, everybody
has that reviewer too, right?

00:48:32.520 --> 00:48:35.700
So as an example, "Gender concordance,

00:48:35.700 --> 00:48:39.540
being the same gender as
your provider, increases

00:48:39.540 --> 00:48:42.270
the patient's probability
of heart attack survival.

00:48:42.270 --> 00:48:46.260
And this effect is driven
only by increased mortality

00:48:46.260 --> 00:48:49.110
when male physicians
treat female patients."

00:48:49.110 --> 00:48:50.850
That's not my favorite quote
from that paper actually.

00:48:50.850 --> 00:48:51.780
That's in the proceedings

00:48:51.780 --> 00:48:53.490
of the National Academy of Sciences.

00:48:53.490 --> 00:48:55.840
My favorite quote is "When male clinicians

00:48:57.240 --> 00:48:59.550
have more female
colleagues they train with,

00:48:59.550 --> 00:49:01.718
they kill fewer female patients".

00:49:01.718 --> 00:49:02.551
(laughing)

00:49:02.551 --> 00:49:05.430
Which is like, are we like
that's a great dose response.

00:49:05.430 --> 00:49:07.410
Does that mean you
should just be surrounded

00:49:07.410 --> 00:49:11.310
by women more like I don't,
it's an interesting effect.

00:49:11.310 --> 00:49:15.750
So this would be like if
male pilots crashed more

00:49:15.750 --> 00:49:17.550
when they were flying
with female passengers,

00:49:17.550 --> 00:49:20.383
which doesn't happen to my knowledge.

00:49:20.383 --> 00:49:21.600
(laughing)

00:49:21.600 --> 00:49:23.890
The second thing is we know that

00:49:25.320 --> 00:49:28.650
Patient-physician-race-match
improves a lot of quality

00:49:28.650 --> 00:49:31.263
of life outcomes and
just adherence outcomes.

00:49:32.430 --> 00:49:33.840
And these are things
that are really important

00:49:33.840 --> 00:49:37.380
to longer term safety and
knowledge about your condition.

00:49:37.380 --> 00:49:39.990
So it'd be like if black
passengers only got

00:49:39.990 --> 00:49:41.730
the in-flight safety announcement,

00:49:41.730 --> 00:49:43.530
if there were a black pilot flying,

00:49:43.530 --> 00:49:46.923
which I again, do not think
happens to my knowledge.

00:49:47.871 --> 00:49:50.850
And the final thing is a
"Majority of Muslim women report

00:49:50.850 --> 00:49:53.910
experiencing poor quality
care and maternity services

00:49:53.910 --> 00:49:57.090
with indicated stereotypical
and discriminatory behavior."

00:49:57.090 --> 00:49:58.140
As somebody with three kids.

00:49:58.140 --> 00:50:01.230
I can say that this is true,
I've experienced it personally,

00:50:01.230 --> 00:50:03.870
but also this would be like if
there were Muslim travel bans

00:50:03.870 --> 00:50:05.580
and Muslim passengers were
always randomly screened.

00:50:05.580 --> 00:50:08.490
This is true, I was randomly
screened on the way here

00:50:08.490 --> 00:50:09.720
for the hundredth times.

00:50:09.720 --> 00:50:14.070
So you know, there are lots of
things that happen in health

00:50:14.070 --> 00:50:16.620
that you would not want to be replicated

00:50:16.620 --> 00:50:18.450
in an aviation setting, right?

00:50:18.450 --> 00:50:20.550
Once we have these sort
of regulatory bodies

00:50:20.550 --> 00:50:22.320
that are involved.

00:50:22.320 --> 00:50:25.050
There are no simple
fixes for AI in health.

00:50:25.050 --> 00:50:27.360
As I said, this is going
to be an ongoing process

00:50:27.360 --> 00:50:29.460
for diverse teams and data.

00:50:29.460 --> 00:50:32.070
But if we consider sources
of bias in the data

00:50:32.070 --> 00:50:34.560
when we select our problems
and collect our data.

00:50:34.560 --> 00:50:37.560
If we evaluate models
comprehensively when we think

00:50:37.560 --> 00:50:40.710
about outcome definitions
and develop our algorithms,

00:50:40.710 --> 00:50:43.530
and if we recognize not
all gaps can be corrected,

00:50:43.530 --> 00:50:46.410
but when you deploy a model,
it can still be useful

00:50:46.410 --> 00:50:50.370
if you're careful about how
the model's advice is given,

00:50:50.370 --> 00:50:54.570
then I think we can actually
work towards better outcomes.

00:50:54.570 --> 00:50:57.900
This is work done by a really
fantastic group of students,

00:50:57.900 --> 00:51:00.360
clinical and technical collaborators,

00:51:00.360 --> 00:51:03.150
and really generous funding sources.

00:51:03.150 --> 00:51:06.090
And I do think that if
we learn robust models,

00:51:06.090 --> 00:51:07.770
audit diverse data,
and deploy fair advice,

00:51:07.770 --> 00:51:09.090
we can create actionable insights

00:51:09.090 --> 00:51:10.440
in human health, thank you.

